 To create a professional-looking 2D plot in LaTeX using TikZ and pgfplots, we can use the following code. The plot will depict a point labeled $x$ being dominated by three other points ($y_1$, $y_2$, $y_3$) with the region to the top right of $x$ shaded pale red and axis-parallel dashed lines (in gray) going through the point $x$.

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{pgfplots}
\usepgfplotslibrary{fillbetween}
\usepackage{xcolor}

\definecolor{paleRed}{RGB}{255,127,140}
\definecolor{gray}{RGB}{128,128,128}

\begin{document}

\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[
    xlabel={$x$},
    ylabel={$y$},
    axis lines=middle,
    xmin=-1, xmax=5,
    ymin=-1, ymax=5,
    enlargelimits={abs=0.5},
    axis line style={latex-latex},
    ticklabel style={font=\small},
    xmajorgrids,
    ymajorgrids,
    grid style={gray!30},
    clip=false,
    ]

% Define the points
\addplot [only marks, mark=*, mark options={fill=black}] coordinates {
    (1,1) coordinate (x) as $x$,
    (2,3) coordinate (y1) as $y_1$,
    (3,2) coordinate (y2) as $y_2$,
    (4,4) coordinate (y3) as $y_3$,
};

% Draw dashed lines through point x
\draw[dashed, gray] (x |- 0,0) -- (x |- axis cs: 0,0);
\draw[dashed, gray] (0,0) -- (axis cs: 0,x);

% Shade region to the top right of x
\addplot [fill=paleRed, forget plot] coordinates {
    (x)
    (axis cs: x,axis cs: x,5)
    (axis cs: 5,axis cs: x,5)
    (axis cs: 5,axis cs: 5,0)
    cycle;
};

\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}

\end{document}
```

This code will produce a professional-looking 2D plot with the desired features. Adjust the coordinates of the points as needed to fit your specific requirements.

 In LaTeX, you can create a new line within a cell in a table by using the `\\` command. Here's a simple example:

```
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{tabularx}

\begin{document}

\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{|X|}
\hline
\textbf{Cell Content} \\
\hline
